# Palisade — Build Memory

## Visual Flair — Atmosphere + Props Layer (2026-06-16, torch system replaced 2026-07-21)

Optional dungeon polish lives entirely in `scripts/VisualFlair.cs`, gated behind a
**control panel** of static fields (master `VisualFlair.Enabled` + per-feature
toggles/values read at build time). Wired in via two guarded one-liners only:
- `DungeonBuilder.Build()` → `VisualFlair.Decorate(this, data.Pieces, TorchByCell)`
- `OnlineDungeonArena.BuildEnvironment()` → `VisualFlair.ApplyEnvironment(env)`

To revert: set `Enabled = false` (runtime), or delete the file + those two calls.

What it does today: exponential depth fog + glow/bloom + exposure grade; layered-sine
flicker via the self-contained `TorchFlicker` node on whatever light `TorchByCell`
holds per cell; tames the raw 3.5-energy Start(green)/Exit(red) landmark glows to
~2.0/1.6; fakes god-ray beams at Start/Exit with an additive cone + spotlight (**true
volumetric fog/SSAO are NOT available in GL Compatibility** — must fake); plus
hanging banners (disabled, `Banners=false`) + floor rubble.

**Torch system replaced 2026-07-21 (Polish Pass Task 2 — playtest: "old torches
still there, remove them, sconces bigger/correctly mounted"):** the old procedural
wall-mounted torch (an ambient `OmniLight3D` from `DungeonBuilder.AddTorches`,
relocated onto a wall by `VisualFlair`'s `WallTorches` flag and paired with a
tiny iron-bracket + emissive-flame-cone mesh via the now-DELETED `AddTorchBracket`)
is gone. GLB sconces (`PieceVariants.AddSconces`, `torch_a`/`torch_b`, scaled to
~1.1m) are now the primary visible torch prop AND the primary light source:
- `AddSconces` places one sconce on every closed wall of every non-stair piece,
  rolls a light 1-in-2 per wall (`Fnv(..., salt: 17) % 2`), and — when lit —
  registers the sconce's own `FlameLight` child (energy 3.0/range 14) directly
  into `DungeonBuilder.TorchByCell` (now threaded through `PieceVariants.Apply`).
- `DungeonBuilder.AddTorches` runs *after* `PieceVariants.Apply` and only adds a
  dim fallback ambient light (2.4/13, no mesh) for a cell that ends up with
  **no** lit sconce — e.g. a 4-way `Cross` piece (no closed wall to mount one on)
  or an unlucky run of unlit rolls — so no cell is ever pitch dark and no cell
  ever double-lights (fallback and sconce-light are mutually exclusive per cell).
  Stair cells and Start/Exit landmarks are untouched — always get their own light,
  same as before.
- `VisualFlair.Decorate`'s regular-corridor branch now ONLY calls
  `flicker?.Register(light, FlickerAmount)` on whatever's in `TorchByCell` — no
  repositioning, no bracket mesh. `WallTorches`/`TorchShadows`/`ShadowEvery`/
  `TorchEnergy`/`TorchRange` fields and `AddTorchBracket`/`WallMount`/`IronMat`/
  `FlameMat` are all deleted (dead once the bracket mesh was removed).
- `TorchSignalSystem` (the Exit→Start BFS reveal, reads `builder.TorchByCell`)
  needed **zero** changes — it only ever needed *a* light per cell, and still
  gets one from either a sconce or the fallback.
- Known minor: if a piece has 2 lit sconces (e.g. a straight corridor with both
  closed walls rolling lit), only the later-registered one gets flicker/gets
  tweened blue by `TorchSignalSystem` — the other stays static. Same trade-off
  the always-lit mid-corridor candelabra already had pre-existing (it was never
  wired into `TorchByCell`/flicker either). Not fixed — matches existing pattern.

Preview harness: `test/TestVisualFlair.cs` (+ `.tscn`) builds a sample corridor and
flies an eye-level camera through it. Pass `-- flairoff` to capture a before/after
from one build. The old `shadowsoff` arg was retired with `TorchShadows`.

